BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Virtual Research Environments: Re-Making St. Paul’s Basilica
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261104T133000
DTSTAMP:20261025T223712Z
UID:dartmouth-83085@free-food-dartmouth
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Original event page(s):\nhttps://home.dartmouth.edu/events/eve
 nt?event=83085\nhttps://dartgo.org/vre-basilica\n\nDescription:\n12:00PM-1
 :30PM\, November 4\, 2026\nKreindler Seminar Room\n031 Haldeman Center\nOp
 en to all attendees\nLunch will be available\nSign up at:\nhttps://dartgo.
 org/vre-basilica\nJoin John Bell (Film and Media Studies\, DREAM Studio) a
 nd Nicola Camerlenghi (Art History) as they discuss their eight-year colla
 boration to produce a virtual research environment for study of architectu
 re and art history.\nVirtual Basilica is a soon-to-be-released desktop and
  virtual reality app dedicated to one of Rome’s most venerable buildings
 \, the Basilica of St. Paul. Built in the late fourth century to enshrine 
 the tomb of the apostle\, it was the last and largest of the imperial basi
 licas in Rome\, surviving until a devastating fire in 1823. Virtual Basili
 ca is based on archival documents\, historical images\, archaeological exc
 avations\, and 3D scans taken from the existing replica of the building\, 
 built after 1823. The app permits users to explore what St. Paul's looked 
 like just prior to the time of the fire\, as well as at key points in its 
 sixteen-hundred-year history.\nBell and Camerlenghi will talk about the pr
 ocess of digitizing and communicating changes in space and appearance over
  time\, as well as what to do when the historical record is not clear. The
  decisions made to produce a virtual environment for an Open Educational R
 esource (OER) intended for students and scholars are much more complex tha
 n just building a 3D model. Through the singular instance of St. Paul’s\
 , Virtual Basilica shows how long-lived buildings can anchor memory and me
 aning across centuries\, persisting precisely because they change with the
  times.\nTeacher plans and student exercises are offered in conjunction wi
 th the app. As an educational resource\, the visualization of the building
  is supported by a database of source information\, and clearly differenti
 ates documented features from those based on informed speculation.\nThe pr
 oject was funded by the National Endowment for the Humanities\, the Mellon
  Foundation\, the Kress Foundation\, as well as by grants from Dartmouth a
 nd Smith Colleges.\n\nSponsor / organizer: Art History Department\, Neukom
  Institute\n\nAudience: Public\n\nSource: Dartmouth\n\nWhy this was includ
 ed: explicit food-service wording\; food keyword near service context\n\nF
 ood availability is inferred from the source listing\; verify details befo
 re attending.
LAST-MODIFIED:20261025T223712Z
LOCATION:Haldeman Center 031 - Kreindler Seminar Room
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
URL:https://home.dartmouth.edu/events/event?event=83085
X-MICROSOFT-CDO-BUSYSTATUS:FREE
